Debian/Ubuntu packaging for the UUID module
==========================================

This directory is the first independent-module packaging example. The native
module and its qpp metadata belong to qore-uuid-module; HTML documentation and
the upstream example test belong to qore-uuid-module-doc. Debhelper generates
qore-uuid-module-dbgsym automatically. JSON, YAML and other modules bundled in
the Qore source tree remain in Qore's own packages.

Build prerequisites and supported scope
--------------------------------------

Install the matching packaged Qore runtime and libqore-dev in an isolated
Debian/Ubuntu build environment, then satisfy debian/control. A /usr/local or
/opt source installation is not a substitute for the packaged SDK. The Qore
package repository and its dependency backports must be configured first.

Qualification currently targets Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 using the locally qualified
Qore 3.0 development packages. Debian and other Ubuntu releases require a
qualified Qore SDK for that distribution and a separate native build/test run.
Do not install the Ubuntu binary on Debian or relabel it for another release.
Cross compilation is deliberately rejected: qpp and Qore execute during builds.

Builds use debhelper compatibility level 13, CMake, system libuuid, distribution
compiler/hardening flags, and ordinary dh_strip automatic debug packages.
UUID has no AOT user modules, so there are no Qore EOF trailers to preserve.
Modules containing AOT user modules must preserve their trailers around stripping
as Qore's debian/preserve-aot-metadata.py does; do not copy UUID's strip behavior
to such modules without adapting it.

Native modules deliberately resolve Qore symbols at load time instead of linking
libqore. Therefore dh_shlibdeps cannot supply that dependency. debian/rules
derives a versioned libqore SONAME package dependency from the installed SDK and
also requires at least that version of qore. Other native library dependencies
come from dh_shlibdeps. The runtime package is not Multi-Arch: same: it requires
the native interpreter and includes shared compiler metadata.

Source and binary builds
------------------------

The snapshot revision in debian/changelog targets the Ubuntu resolute testing
PPA. Change its version and distribution for another target, and use a new
revision for every changed source package after upload. A future upstream release must sort after the development
snapshot (for example, 1.4.3 sorts after 1.4.2+git20260928.1).

From a clean committed checkout, prepare an original source archive without
debian/ and extract it beside a copy containing debian/. For this snapshot:

    version=1.4.2+git20260928.1
    mkdir -p ../uuid-package-work
    git archive --format=tar --prefix="qore-uuid-module-$version/" \
        HEAD -- . ':!debian' | xz > \
        "../uuid-package-work/qore-uuid-module_$version.orig.tar.xz"
    tar -xf "../uuid-package-work/qore-uuid-module_$version.orig.tar.xz" \
        -C ../uuid-package-work
    cp -a debian "../uuid-package-work/qore-uuid-module-$version/"
    cd "../uuid-package-work/qore-uuid-module-$version"
    dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc

Feed the .dsc to sbuild or another clean native build environment with all
Build-Depends preinstalled. Extract the .dsc with dpkg-source -x, then run as
an unprivileged user with networking disabled:

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

The upstream suite runs against the newly built module. DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS may
contain parallel=N, noopt, nostrip or nocheck. Both DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nodoc and
DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=nodoc suppress documentation generation; the nodoc profile
also omits the documentation package. An architecture-only build uses -B and
does not require Doxygen. Do not use nocheck for a release qualification.

Qualification and publication
-----------------------------

Run Lintian on the source and binary .changes. In a fresh disposable testbed,
run autopkgtest on the source and built runtime/documentation packages; its
runtime test loads only the installed UUID module, and its compiler test uses
the installed SDK to compile and execute a program with named UUID arguments.
Neither test needs external services. Also verify runtime-only installation
without libqore-dev, coinstallation of documentation and matching debug symbols,
dpkg -V, purge/reinstall, and byte-identical packages from two distinct build
directories. Keep package hashes, SDK versions, logs, and the source commit.

Local qualification does not publish anything. Release uploads require the
final Qore ABI decision, supported distribution/architecture qualification,
source signing, and an explicit release/publication step. The generated minimum
runtime dependency may refer to a local development package until that qualified
Qore version is available in the target archive.

For the next module, inspect its native libraries, Qore module requirements,
user modules/resources, generated metadata, optional features, third-party
licenses and service-dependent tests before adapting this example.
